@@ -13,12 +13,42 @@ Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due
 
 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend uses a **local embedded SQLite index** built from each validated snapshot. The index is rebuilt during import and activated atomically with the snapshot. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of the backend choice.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Build the SQLite index from the validated records in a staging directory. Activate the new snapshot and its index only after all validation and index build succeed. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot and index active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. Lookups query the active SQLite index using a parameterized SELECT on the normalized serial number column. The index is read-only during normal operation; no writes occur except during snapshot import.
+
+## Index Schema
+
+The SQLite index uses a single table with these columns:
+- `serial_norm` (TEXT, PRIMARY KEY) — normalized serial number (whitespace trimmed, ASCII uppercased)
+- `serial_raw` (TEXT) — original serial number as it appears in the snapshot
+- `calibration_date` (TEXT) — ISO 8601 date
+- `due_date` (TEXT) — ISO 8601 date
+- `description` (TEXT) — equipment description
+- `snapshot_date` (TEXT) — ISO 8601 date of the snapshot this record belongs to
+
+An index covers exactly one snapshot. The `snapshot_date` column is denormalized into every row so a lookup can return the snapshot date without a join.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; Managed search service | Predictable offline lookups, no service to run, no network dependency during operation. Index rebuild cost stays inside the registry component and is bounded by snapshot size (≤150 MB). |
+| Index rebuild strategy | Full rebuild in staging directory, atomic swap | Incremental update; In-place rebuild | Simplicity and correctness: a full rebuild from validated records guarantees consistency. Staging + atomic swap satisfies the tenet that a failed import leaves the last valid snapshot available. |
+| Index schema | Single denormalized table | Normalized schema with separate snapshot table | Lookup latency target (≤250 ms) favors a single-index seek. Denormalizing `snapshot_date` into every row avoids a join and keeps the query plan trivial. 90k rows × 6 columns is ~10 MB, well within kiosk RAM. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Lookup backend: local embedded SQLite index (recorded above)
+
+### Deferred
+1. SQLite WAL mode vs. rollback journal for read concurrency during import staging
+2. Whether to vacuum the index after build (snapshot size is bounded, likely unnecessary)
+3. Exact serialization format for the staged index file (SQLite file vs. SQL dump) — decided by implementation
